Because `task` is a **built-in Deep Agents delegation tool** that gets added automatically when subagents are available. You did not need to put it inside `tools=[...]`. ([GitHub][1])

In our code:

```python
agent = create_deep_agent(
    model="openai:gpt-5.5",
    subagents=[risk_analyst],
)
```

this:

```python
subagents=[risk_analyst]
```

causes Deep Agents to add a delegation middleware internally, and that middleware exposes:

```text
task
```

to the main agent. The `task` tool accepts things like:

```text
description
subagent_type
```

So the flow is:

```text
create_deep_agent(...)
        ↓
subagents are provided
        ↓
SubAgentMiddleware is added
        ↓
Deep Agents creates built-in task tool
        ↓
Main model can call task(...)
        ↓
risk-analyst is invoked
```

Also, this part:

```python
"tools": []
```

inside:

```python
risk_analyst = {
    ...
    "tools": []
}
```

means:

> the **risk-analyst subagent itself has no additional tools**.

It does **not** mean that the main agent has no `task` tool.

So there are really two different tool levels:

```text
MAIN AGENT
│
└── task          ← automatically added by Deep Agents
       ↓
    risk-analyst
       │
       └── tools=[]   ← this subagent has no tools
```

That is why you saw:

```text
Tool Name: task
Tool Input: {...}
```

even though we never manually wrote:

```python
tools=[task]
```

Deep Agents created it for us automatically.

